{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "עקב", :translit "akev", :gloss "heel"} {:he "הגדיל עלי עקב", :translit "higdil alai akev", :gloss "he has magnified against me heel"} {:he "והוא יגוד עקב", :translit "vehu yegud akev", :gloss "and he shall bruise heel"} {:he "חבל על שמש גדול שאבד מן העולם", :translit "chaval al shemesh gadol she'avad min ha'olam", :gloss "woe for the great luminary that was lost from the world"} {:he "גם איש שלומי", :translit "gam ish shlomi", :gloss "also the man of my peace"} {:he "איש אשר כברכתו ברך אותם", :translit "ish asher kivrachto berach otam", :gloss "each man according to his blessing he blessed them"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Word עקב appears three times in Scripture: Genesis 3:15, Job 19:11, and Psalms 41:10"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'woe for the great luminary that was lost from the world' is applied to the serpent in Bava Batra 75a and Sanhedrin 38b. The phrase 'also the man of my peace' (Psalms 41:10) is connected there to the serpent's loss of greatness. Regarding the tribal blessings, Jacob compared Judah to a lion (Genesis 49:9) and Naphtali to a hind (Genesis 49:21), yet the verse 'each man according to his blessing he blessed them' (Genesis 49:28) teaches he equated all tribes. Jacob compared Dan to a serpent (Genesis 49:17), yet Gad too was included in that same blessing.", :sources ["Bava Batra 75a" "Sanhedrin 38b" "Genesis 49:9" "Genesis 49:17" "Genesis 49:21" "Genesis 49:28" "Psalms 41:10" "Job 19:11"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Word עקב appears three times in Scripture: Genesis 3:15, Job 19:11, and Psalms 41:10", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:04:16.106368501Z", :model "glm-5.2"}